																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-89000/help:ideas-critique">Writing Help / Help: Ideas Critique</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13865362/freddy-s-fishman-seeking-greenlight-critique">Freddy&#039;s Fishman (seeking Greenlight &amp; Critique)</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4835125">
									

<p><strong>Summoned via PM:</strong></p>
<p>Hi there Dr Elliot Anemone. Apologies for the delay in getting this critique to you. Let’s take a look at your concept.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;crit</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>While the idea sounds interesting, and the new, proposed GoI has potential, I feel your Central Narrative has a couple of convenient conjectures that don’t really hold up to scrutiny. I’ll list a couple below for you to iron out:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>after something interfered with the communications between the foundation and the agents</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What interfered with the communications? Is this crucial to the story, or simply a way for you (the author) to get the MTF captured? Speaking of being captured, what happens to the MTF? You quickly go into <em>another</em> set of Foundation agents who discover the scp – why not simply start your discovery log at this point? Was there a need for the MTF to go in first?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Doctor Josef Monstrosity thanking the foundation for finding their med students projects, and left this one as a show of thanks.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Why would this gesture be “a show of thanks”?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>attempts by the Foundation to gain the Fishmans trust in hopes that it is capable of telling the Foundation where it came from</p>
</blockquote>
<p>If it were experimented on by the medical students, wouldn’t the Fishman automatically side with the Foundation? Why the resistance to give up a place and people who tortured it?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Dr. Franklin, a young researcher with vague origins, but was brought in by the higher ups so staff just decide it's best not to question it</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Again, this sounds dubious at best, unprofessional at worst. You’ve introduced a character to advance the story, and nothing else. There’s no reason for his inclusion at this point unless you have a good explanation for his appearance.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>how it went to the hospital willingly at first because when it was human it had aquagenic urticaria and the hospital said they could cure him</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a good motivation for the scip to have, and an excellent introduction to what the new GoI is capable / not capable of doing. The problem is you seem to have given us conflicting views of what the GoI actually is: is it a place for so-called monsters to go to get cured or experimented on?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>how the Hospital views normalcy as an illness that needs to be remedied.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This, then, becomes even more problematic as you have an additional goal for the GoI. Why was it experimenting on the fish-man then? Wouldn’t it view all mutants as normal people?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Lots of plot inconsistencies I hope you can help clear up. If you decide to revise the concept / respond below, do drop me another PM and I’ll do my best to take another look. For now, all the best!</p>

							</div>

								
					
